{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB11978 - MONDO_5224",
  "verbalization": "Glasdegib is implicated in the treatment of Acute Myeloblastic Leukemia Without Maturation (AML-NM), as evidenced by its indication for AML with Npm1 somatic mutations, inv3(p21;q26.2), or T(3;3)(p21;q26.2), all of which are genetic alterations commonly observed in AML-NM. The shared pathway involving Mitoxantrone, a chemotherapy agent, further supports a mechanistic link, as Mitoxantrone is known to induce apoptosis in leukemic cells. The high scores from the three paths suggest a strong, consistent relationship between Glasdegib and AML-NM, likely through its interaction with the mitotic process or other cellular pathways that are dysregulated in AML. The presence of multiple genetic subtypes under the same indication indicates that Glasdegib may target a broader spectrum of AML-NM subtypes, potentially through a common mechanism. The integration of these pathways and the shared use of Mitoxantrone underscores the potential for Glasdegib to be a repurposed therapy for AML-NM, provided the underlying mechanism is robust and well-characterized.",
